{"id":"vanilla-rnn-cell","t":"Elman RNN over padded sequences","tags":["Neural Networks","NLP","Time Series"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"rnn_forward","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"A vanilla (Elman) RNN reads a sequence one step at a time, carrying a hidden state:\n\n$$h_t = \\tanh(x_t W_x + h_{t-1} W_h + b).$$\n\nBatches mix sequences of different lengths, padded to a common $T$. Padding steps must not change a sequence's state.\n\nWrite `rnn_forward(x, h0, Wx, Wh, b, lengths=None)` that returns `(outputs, h_last)`.\n\n## Shapes (batch first)\n- `x`: `(N, T, D)`; `h0`: `(N, H)`; `Wx`: `(D, H)`; `Wh`: `(H, H)`; `b`: `(H,)`.\n- `lengths`: int array `(N,)` with $0 \\le$ `lengths[n]` $\\le T$, or `None` meaning every sequence has length $T$.\n\n## Output\n- `outputs`: `(N, T, H)`. For sample $n$, row $t$ is $h_t$ for $t <$ `lengths[n]` and **zeros** for padded steps.\n- `h_last`: `(N, H)`, each sample's state after its **last valid** step (`h0[n]` itself if its length is 0).","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef rnn_forward(x, h0, Wx, Wh, b, lengths=None):\n    \"\"\"x: (N, T, D); h0: (N, H). Return (outputs (N, T, H), h_last (N, H)).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["The input projection $x_t W_x + b$ doesn't depend on the state, so compute it for every step with one product before the loop. Only $h_{t-1} W_h$ must be sequential.","At step $t$ a sequence is still running iff `t < lengths[n]`. Make that a `(N, 1)` boolean column.","Use it twice with `np.where`: to keep the old state for finished sequences, and to write zeros into their output rows. Then `h` after the loop is already each sample's last valid state."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef rnn_forward(x, h0, Wx, Wh, b, lengths=None):\n    x, h = np.asarray(x, dtype=float), np.asarray(h0, dtype=float)\n    N, T, _ = x.shape\n    lengths = np.full(N, T) if lengths is None else np.asarray(lengths)\n    xw = x @ Wx + b                                    # input projections for all steps at once\n    out = np.zeros((N, T, h.shape[1]))\n    for t in range(T):                                 # the recurrence itself is sequential\n        h_new = np.tanh(xw[:, t] + h @ Wh)\n        live = (t < lengths)[:, None]                  # which sequences are still running\n        h = np.where(live, h_new, h)                   # padded steps keep the old state\n        out[:, t] = np.where(live, h_new, 0.0)\n    return out, h\n","ex_md":"## What can be parallel, what can't\n$x_t W_x$ for all $t$ is one big matrix product, and frameworks do exactly that (cuDNN fuses it). The $h_{t-1} W_h$ term creates a dependency chain of length $T$, which is the fundamental reason RNNs train slower than transformers on long sequences.\n\n## Masking\nFreezing the state on padded steps makes the result for a sequence independent of how much padding it got, so `h_last` is its true final state. This is what `pack_padded_sequence` achieves in PyTorch. Without it, a short sequence keeps evolving on padding inputs (often zeros, which still change $h$ through $h W_h + b$).\n\n## Vanishing and exploding gradients\n$\\partial h_t/\\partial h_{t-1} = \\operatorname{diag}(1 - h_t^2)\\,W_h^\\top$. Backprop through $k$ steps multiplies $k$ of these, so gradients shrink or blow up geometrically depending on the spectral radius of $W_h$. Clipping handles the explosion; gating (LSTM, GRU) handles the vanishing.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(N T (DH + H^2))$ time, $O(NTH)$ memory to keep the outputs for backprop.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Writing $W_h h$ with row-vector states: shapes only work by accident when $W_h$ is square, and the result is $W_h^\\top$.\n- Returning `outputs[:, -1]` as the last state: that's a zero row for any padded sequence.\n- Off-by-one in the mask (`t <= lengths`).\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Derive backprop through time for this cell and say where truncated BPTT cuts the graph.\n- How does a bidirectional RNN combine states, and why can't it be used for autoregressive generation?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[[1.],\n        [0.]]])\narray([[0.]])\narray([[1.]])\narray([[1.]])\narray([0.])","out":"(array([[[0.76159416],\n        [0.64201499]]]), array([[0.64201499]]))"},{"in":"array([[[1.],\n        [2.]],\n\n       [[1.],\n        [5.]]])\narray([[0.],\n       [0.]])\narray([[0.5]])\narray([[1.]])\narray([0.])\narray([2, 1])","out":"(array([[[0.46211716],\n        [0.89806301]],\n\n       [[0.46211716],\n        [0.        ]]]), array([[0.89806301],\n       [0.46211716]]))"}],"lessons":[{"href":"../ai-research-track-web/index.html#l04-24","t":"Natural Language Processing","c":"AI Research"},{"href":"../ai-research-track-web/index.html#l04-27","t":"Time-Series Prediction","c":"AI Research"},{"href":"../deep-learning/index.html#l05-01","t":"Recurrent networks","c":"Deep Learning"}],"related":["gru-cell","lstm-cell","sequence-to-sequence-forward-pass"],"ntests":7,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[[1.0], [0.0]]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[[0.7615941559557649], [0.6420149920119997]]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.6420149920119997]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[[1.0], [2.0]], [[1.0], [5.0]]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.0], [0.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.5]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [2, 1], \"dtype\": \"int64\"}], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [[[0.46211715726000974], [0.8980630114608957]], [[0.46211715726000974], [0.0]]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [[0.8980630114608957], [0.46211715726000974]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[[-0.5667637694627934, -1.056574181365729, 1.0620799269660266, -1.1293521644619289], [-1.779405